#!/bin/bash
# 696 (Sat 21 Nov 2026 08:45, Peter: "look at what wins and then work from there. You are not following the brief").
# Builder session 7: start from the winning trades that really existed in every recorded market, then work back to what could
# be seen beforehand. Waits for any running builder session. Paper only. No orders. Nothing deleted.
B=/root/pe-builder
cat > $B/BRIEF_winners.md <<'BRIEF'
You are the Builder on Peter Edwards' trading server, session 7. Started once. Do the job, leave ordinary background processes running, write your handover, stop. No idle loops; long runs in the background with nohup + nice.
FIRST read /root/pe-queue/reports/builder_handover_4.md, builder_handover_5.md, builder_handover_6.md if present, and /root/pe-builder/BRIEF_league.md (Peter's instruction and the HARD RULES apply unchanged: paper only, never an order endpoint, nothing deleted, back up before editing). Reuse /root/pe-sports/grid/ (onetest.py fills, cache.py, facts.py).
PETER'S ORDER, in his words: "look at what wins and then work from there." Until now we wrote rules first and tested them forward; 8,663 rules later the paper is losing. Turn it round. Start from the money that was really there.
STEP 1 - WHAT WON (table grid.winners, every recorded day 31 Oct to now, every sport and market we record): for each market and selection find every in-and-out trade that would REALLY have made money at real prices: enter by taking the price on offer (money shown, £2 minimum, up to £50), exit by taking the price on offer later, profit after the gap both ways and 2% commission, position open between 30 seconds and 60 minutes, both ends before the result is known (pre-start to pre-start, pre-start to in-play, in-play to in-play). Keep the non-overlapping best ones per selection with at least £1 profit on a £50 stake, with: entry and exit time and price, side, minutes open, minutes to or from the start, money available at each end. Do the same for offering (enter by resting an order that a later trade goes THROUGH) as a separate kind. This is hindsight on purpose: it is the map of where the money was, not a result.
STEP 2 - THE MAP (reports/winners_map.md, plain English, two screens at most): where the winning trades were - by sport, market type, time relative to the start, price band, back-first or lay-first, minutes open, league or course, size of the market; how many per day, how much money a £50 trader could have taken per day if perfect, and how concentrated it is (a few matches or spread). Name the ten kinds of situation that hold most of the money, each in one plain sentence.
STEP 3 - WHAT COULD BE SEEN BEFORE (the point of the exercise): for every winner and for a matched set of moments where no winning trade followed (same sport, market type, time and price band), collect only what was known BEFORE the entry: the price path over the previous 30 s, 2, 10 and 60 minutes; the gap between back and lay; money waiting on each side and its change; money traded in the last minute and its direction; the other markets of the same event (their prices and whether they have already moved); the other exchanges; time to the start or minute of play; score, shots, shots on target and expected goals where a fact was already received (facts.py known-at rule); for racing: favourite or not, field size, position in the betting, draw where known. Then find which of these separate winners from non-winners, using ONLY the first half of the days; simple splits first (one or two conditions a person could say in a sentence), no black boxes.
STEP 4 - CHECK, do not believe: every condition found in step 3 becomes a rule with an entry AND an exit both fixed from what is known at the time (the exit is a rule too: a target, a time limit and a stop), and is run through the one test on the SECOND half of the days, which it has never seen. Report both halves side by side. Anything that holds goes into earn_ways as a TRADE and onto paper like any other row. Expect most of step 3 to vanish in step 4; say so plainly with the numbers.
STEP 5 - /root/pe-queue/reports/builder_handover_7.md, under 30 lines, plain English: line 1 = how much perfect-hindsight money a £50 trader had per day and where most of it sat; line 2 = how many conditions separated winners on the first half and how many survived the second half, with £ kept per £100 on each half; then the surviving rules one sentence each; what was checked (no fact or price after the entry is used: hand-worked checks in test_known.py); what could not be done. Commit and push: cd /root/pe-queue && git add reports/builder_handover_7.md reports/winners_map.md reports/league_table.md 2>/dev/null; git commit -qm "builder session 7: start from what won" && git pull -q --no-rebase --no-edit && env -u GITHUB_TOKEN git push -q. Then end the session.
BRIEF
cat > $B/run_builder_winners.sh <<'SH'
#!/bin/bash
CL=$(cat /root/pe-watchman/claude_path); ts=$(date -u +%F_%H%M)
cd /root/pe-sports/grid && timeout -k 60 21600 "$CL" -p "$(cat /root/pe-builder/BRIEF_winners.md)" --allowedTools "Bash,Read,Write,Edit,Glob,Grep" --max-turns 600 --output-format text > /root/pe-builder/runs/${ts}_winners.log 2>&1
echo "builder winners session ended $(date -u '+%F %H:%M') exit $?" >> /root/pe-builder/sessions.log
SH
chmod +x $B/run_builder_winners.sh
if pgrep -f '[r]un_builder' >/dev/null; then echo "a builder session is still running - waiting"; exit 75; else nohup setsid $B/run_builder_winners.sh > /dev/null 2>&1 < /dev/null & sleep 40; echo "builder session 7 started: $(pgrep -fc '[r]un_builder_winners.sh')"; fi
